                  <msItem>
                     <title>Epp. Jeronimi</title>
                     <note type="MLGB3_generalNotes">The manuscript is reported by M. M.
                        Breccia-Fratadocchi, 'Da York a Roma: per la storia del codice Vitt. Em. 827
                        della Biblioteca Nazionale Centrale', Archivio della Società Romana di
                        Storia Patria 117 (1994), 97--111.</note>
                  </msItem>
               </msContents>
               <history>
                  <origin>
                     <origDate when-custom="115X/1175" notBefore="1150" notAfter="1175" calendar="#gregorian">s. xii 3/4</origDate>
                  </origin>
                  <provenance type="#e"><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_608">York, Yorkshire.
                        Benedictine abbey of Saint Mary the Virgin</orgName><p>On fol. 1r is the
                        ex libris 'Liber monasterii Beate Marie Ebor'' (s. xiv).</p><p>On the same
                        page is the abbey's press-mark: 'In ff.18'. (Note that this should not be
                        taken to be the press-mark of Hereford OFM, as was assumed in New
                        Paleographical Society, First series, II (London, 1903--12), table 17, n.
                        6.)</p><q type="pressmark">In ff.18</q></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Joseph Barrois, with the catalog number
                     '345'.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Purchased in 1849 by Bertram Ashburnham, 4th
                     earl of Ashburnham (1797--1878), with other MSS from the Barrois collection;
                     his catalogue number '271' on the inner front board.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Sold at Sotheby's, 10 June 1901, to Charles
                     Fairfax Murray (1849--1919).</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Quickly thence to George Dunn, fol. i verso:
                     'STB G.D. may 1905'; also bookplate: 'From the library of George Dunn of
                     Woolley Hall near Maidenhead".</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Sold at Sotheby's, 11 February 1913, to the
                     London bookseller John Leighton.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Sold to the Italian book dealer Basilio
                     Benedetti, between 1913 and 1916, and finally purchased by the Biblioteca
                     Nazionale Centrale di Roma, in 1916, for 3000 lire.</provenance>
               </history>
